From 94b97ce9e7f96d35c2cc626f97310a776a791e53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 02:27:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 103/106] Aurora: in-between frames off the translation worker,
 at a steady cadence

On a Windows PC (i9-13900KF, RTX 5090) Smooth Motion held Wind Waker below
30 FPS on Outset: the village is about 10,000 draws a frame, and the FIFO
translation worker, which the game waits for at every frame's end, was
saturated, the in-between frame's matching and blending a fifth of it.

- Draws are matched and blended on a helper thread, in draw order, while
  the translation worker goes on. Each draw records its job (with whether it
  repeats the draw before's constants); end_frame waits for the helper, and
  the render worker reads each job's range while it encodes the in-between
  frame. A traced frame still matches on the recording thread.
- The in-between staging is appended rather than cleared and copied over, and
  its de-duplication compares against the staged block instead of keeping a
  second copy. The per-frame key table keeps its size instead of regrowing
  through a dozen rehashes each frame.
- An unchanged texture takes one hash lookup instead of two: its handle is
  kept with its address state (the lookup by the whole key was a tenth of the
  worker on Outset).
- A frame with nothing to blend (a menu, a still shot, a cut) keeps the
  in-between cadence: the frame on screen is shown again, and this one half a
  frame later. Presenting it at once put it on screen half a frame early at
  every cut, and the frame rate read 30 in every menu.
